@@ -46,6 +46,20 @@ released.
 
 ## Configuration
 
+Set `realtime.mode: "talk"` to use the same conversation, provider, voice,
+instructions, reasoning, tools, and permissions as the OpenClaw app. This mode
+uses the existing authenticated Talk Gateway client APIs. It attaches to the
+configured main session without a custom Gateway carrier runtime. Configure only the
+carrier's owner handles and optional Talk session key; provider, model, voice,
+instruction, and tool-policy overrides belong in Talk configuration.
+
+Normal hangup stops audio and closes call resources, while accepted agent work
+follows native Talk completion into the shared conversation. Explicit task
+cancellation remains separate. The existing FaceTime tool handles a delegated request to end the call.
+
+The default `realtime.mode: "standalone"` preserves the published carrier
+configuration described below. It does not provide the shared Talk behavior.
+
 `ownerHandles` is the only caller list. Every accepted identity receives owner
 authority. `realtime.toolPolicy` is one of `safe-read-only`, `owner`, or `none`;
 invalid explicit values fail validation.
@@ -83,3 +97,19 @@ excluded from the package. See [THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md](THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md).
 The separately released Objective-C and Swift native sources, their adapted
 third-party notices, and their signing pipeline live in
 `openclaw/openclaw-facetime`.
+
+## Stable audio consent
+
+Runtime setup stages the vendor-signed capture helper at a stable physical path
+in `~/Library/Application Support/OpenClaw/FaceTime/audio`. A small native
+launcher makes that helper its own macOS audio-permission identity, independent
+of Node and Homebrew's versioned installation paths. The helper is not re-signed.
+Setup builds the launcher with the already-required Xcode tools only when its
+source or the installed helper changes. Subsequent starts reuse the staged files.
+
+The launcher replaces its own process, preserving the capture PID, standard
+streams, and shutdown controls. It accepts only capture, `--check`, and
+`--default-devices`; unsupported macOS responsibility APIs fail explicitly.
+Normal macOS consent is still required. A successful process-tap startup is not
+proof of consent or audible audio. Node upgrade and native-helper upgrade
+persistence require separate acceptance checks.
